<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
        <RootNamespace>Vixen.Live.Orchestration</RootNamespace>
        <Description>
            The megaserver's intelligence: the hard filters and the score that decide which shard a
            player is placed on, the explanation of why, and the hysteresis that decides when a map
            grows a shard and when it gives one back.
        </Description>
        <PackageTags>mmo;orchestration;placement;megaserver;vixen</PackageTags>
        <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Live.Abstractions\Vixen.Live.Abstractions.csproj" />

        <!--
          `.vxplacement` is YAML, and doc 27 § Placement is explicit that the weights are a thing a
          game authors rather than constants the engine ships. This is the reader for one — ⚠ and
          nothing in the tree calls it outside this project's tests, so a game that wants authored
          weights reads the file itself and hands the record to `MapOptions`. Both halves of the
          asset form are owed, not just the editor-side importer; see the README.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Yaml\Vixen.Core.Yaml.csproj" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Reflection\Vixen.Core.Reflection.csproj" />

        <!--
          The descriptors the YAML binder reads a member through. `PlacementWeights` is a
          `[DataContract]`, and an assembly holding one has to run the generator that describes it —
          the binder refuses a type the registry does not know rather than reflecting over it, which
          is what makes the same code work on a trimmed build.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Vixen.Core.Reflection.Generator\Vixen.Core.Reflection.Generator.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
          ⚠ The grains, and nothing about hosting one. `Microsoft.Orleans.Server` is what a silo
          needs; this assembly holds implementations that a silo loads, and the executable that
          stands the silo up is the orchestrator head rather than this library. Keeping the two
          apart is what lets a test host a grain in-process without a cluster.

          Note what is NOT referenced back: `PlacementDirector` and `MapFleet` know nothing about
          Orleans and are compiled here beside the grains that use them. That is the ordering the
          project was built in and it is worth preserving — the intelligence is testable without a
          scheduler, and the grain is the scheduler.
        -->
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.Server" />
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Vixen.Live.Cluster\Vixen.Live.Cluster.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Vixen.Live.Orchestrator.Tests" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
